# The bench profile: the radio settings this desk needs, IN GIT.
#
# ⚠️ THIS FILE EXISTS BECAUSE THE BENCH CONFIGURATION WAS LOST ON 2026-08-17.
# `sdkconfig` is generated and gitignored, and the settings that made this rig
# work -- both leaves pinned to 8.5 dBm with the power sweep off -- lived ONLY in
# those generated files. TEC-NATKIT-49 required `rm build/<target>-<role>/sdkconfig`
# (which build-role.sh itself instructs), the configs regenerated from the committed
# defaults, and the radio silently reverted to "sweep on, power whatever IDF likes".
# One leaf was reflashed and the other was not, so the two nodes then ran DIFFERENT
# radio configurations and the resulting per-node discrepancy was measured for an
# hour before anyone checked what was actually configured.
#
# So: anything about this bench's radio that is not an ESP-IDF default belongs
# here, not in a generated file.
#
# Use it with:
#     NATKIT_PROFILE=bench ./build-role.sh leaf esp32 -p /dev/ttyACM1 flash
#
# ⚠️⚠️ LEAVES ONLY. DO NOT BUILD THE PRIMARY WITH THIS.
# The documented bench state pins the LEAVES; the primary's power was never
# pinned, and pinning it is actively harmful. Measured 2026-08-17, same boards,
# minutes apart:
#     primary pinned to 8.5 dBm   both leaves degraded, 14.4% and 8.1% loss,
#                                 ~40% of beacons missed at each
#     primary at full power       one leaf at 0 gaps and 99.1 fresh samples/s
# The hub's beacons are BROADCAST -- no acknowledgement, no retry -- so a beacon
# that is not heard is simply lost, and every leaf's clock fit is built from them.
# Turning the hub down costs every node at once. The "lower is better" lesson on
# this bench is about a LEAF overloading the hub's receiver; it does not run in
# reverse.
#
# ⚠️ THIS IS A BENCH PROFILE, NOT A DEPLOYMENT ONE. Nodes here sit centimetres
# apart; deployments have them metres apart and want the sweep doing its job.
# Do not move these into sdkconfig.defaults.

# --- Transmit power ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# 8.5 dBm (34 quarter-dBm), pinned, sweep OFF.
#
# ⚠️ PINNED RATHER THAN SWEPT ON PURPOSE. The sweep picks a level PER NODE at
# every boot by argmax on acknowledgement rate, and two nodes landing on very
# different powers is what produced the original "near-far problem" -- the far
# node arrives 15-25 dB weaker and is squeezed out of the hub's receiver. With
# both pinned to the same level they arrive within about 1 dB.
#
# ⚠️ AND HIGHER IS NOT BETTER HERE. At a few centimetres free-space loss is only
# ~10 dB, so 19.5 dBm arrives at roughly +9 dBm -- far above any 2.4 GHz front
# end's linear range. Measured on this bench: turning both radios DOWN from 19.5
# to 2 dBm took the hub from 0.4 to 8.5 received frames/s. 8.5 dBm is the level
# this rig has been running at since 2026-08-14; if the discrepancy in
# TEC-NATKIT-37 turns out to be receiver overload, the next thing to try is 8
# (2.0 dBm), which is what fixed it before.
CONFIG_NATKIT_TX_POWER_SWEEP=n
CONFIG_NATKIT_TX_POWER_QUARTER_DBM=34

# --- Channel: already committed, deliberately NOT repeated here ---------------
#
# CONFIG_NATKIT_CHANNEL_SURVEY=n and CONFIG_NATKIT_ESPNOW_CHANNEL=3 are already in
# sdkconfig.defaults, so they survived the incident above and are not restated
# here -- two sources of truth for one setting is how they drift apart. Only
# settings that DIFFER from the committed defaults belong in a profile.
